<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#Emotion> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#promptExtractionText> "Extract Emotion Expression elements from the image. Emotion Expression includes: facial expressions, emotional gestures, mood indicators, affective elements, and emotional cues. For each emotional element found, provide: 1) A descriptive instance_name (e.g., 'mischievous_smile', 'concerned_expression'), 2) A natural language label (e.g., 'the mischievous smile', 'the concerned expression'), 3) A brief description of the emotional state or expression. Focus on clearly visible emotional indicators that convey feelings or moods. Example output format: {\"instance_name\": \"mischievous_smile\", \"label\": \"the mischievous smile\", \"description\": \"A knowing, slightly devious smile suggesting mischief or satisfaction\"}"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01247#racism> <http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#extractedFrom> "01247.png" .
<http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#modallyContradicting> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#domain> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Thing> .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01247#dimension_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "the overallintent"@en .
<http://w3c.org/memes-ontology/general/01247#semiotic_projection_1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The user is projected onto the textual material 'you can't be racist if there is no other race' as the central point of engagement. The meme's satirical intent relies on the viewer (user) interpreting the text's irony and recognizing the historical context of Hitler to understand the critique of racism. The user is not explicitly addressed with deictic pronouns like 'you' or 'me' in the text, but the rhetorical structure of the statement implicitly positions the viewer as the one who must confront the absurdity of the claim, making the user the implied subject of the ironic projection."@en .
